{"abstract":"This paper explores the visual echo chamber effect in online climate change communication. We analyze communication by progressive actors and counteractors involved in the public debate about climate change on Facebook, to address the possibility that visual content can bridge ideologically diverse communities. Specifically, we investigate whether visual content depicting protest serves this purpose. The findings reveal a small amount of shared visual content. Interestingly, the emotional reactions to this content for the most part diverge significantly, suggesting that pre-existing attitudes, such as climate ideological position, influence interpretation. Contrary to our expectations, however, we do not observe visual content representing protest activity bridging the two groups. This work posits the possibility of a two-fold (de)polarization around visual content that both connects and divides, which contributes to a more nuanced understanding of the social dynamics that create and sustain the echo chamber effect observed in online climate change debates.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Rossi, Luca","Arminio, Luigi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776440"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Climate change","Polarization","Social media","Visual communication"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"do you see what i see emotional reaction to visual content in the online debate about climate change","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17524032.2024.2420787","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776440","identifier":[{"id":"85209660335","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ae8d2f6a-0d20-4fe6-825d-54f2ef6a877a"],"keyword":["climate change","polarization","social media","visual communication"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E7685B385DE0B1D6076A279FC4959BA5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/epdf/10.1080/17524032.2024.2420787?needAccess=true","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17524032.2024.2420787","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"rossipau","last":"Rossi","locid":"1b45e3f5-b8cd-4c0d-a444-2901304f76ee","name":"Rossi, Luca","orcid":"0000-0002-3629-2039","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luigi","key":"arminiopau","last":"Arminio","locid":"cfb0029a-04d0-4606-810f-bf6d6777171b","name":"Arminio, Luigi","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"]},"sid":["itu-ae8d2f6a-0d20-4fe6-825d-54f2ef6a877a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Do You See What I See? Emotional Reaction to Visual Content in the Online Debate About Climate Change","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776440","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
